CHAPTER X
GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION
Article 28
(1) The Regent conducts guidance and supervision of the implementation of women's empowerment and protection.
(2) The forms of guidance as referred to in paragraph (1) are as follows:
a. providing socialization to the community and business sector regarding;
b. policies on women's empowerment and protection;
c. providing books, leaflets, brochures regarding women's empowerment and protection, and other related issues as well as disseminating them to the community;
d. facilitating capacity building of institutions organizing women's empowerment and protection;
e. facilitating forums for women's participation and women's organizations;
f. facilitating rehabilitation and reintegration services for women victims of disasters and victims of violence;
g. coordinating the participation of the business sector and other institutions, including international assistance for women's empowerment and protection; and
h. providing awards to the community and business sector both individually and institutionally who have made efforts in women's empowerment and protection.
(3) The form of supervision as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of monitoring and evaluation activities on women's empowerment and protection.

Article 14
(1) The implementation of protection for female workers/laborers as referred to in Article 13 letter a is to provide protection from acts of economic exploitation and cruel, inhumane treatment and to ensure proper appreciation for their work.
(2) Female workers/laborers as referred to in paragraph (1) receive protection for:
a. recognition of rights, wages and decent working conditions;
b. health and social security;
c. access to information and legal consultation services;
d. opportunities to obtain knowledge and skills to improve status; and
e. witnesses and victims.

Article 17
(1) The implementation of protection for homeless women as referred to in Article 13 letter d is in the context of providing protection and prevention against forms of violence caused by the absence of shelter or permanent residence.
(2) Homeless women as referred to in paragraph (1) receive the right to protection for:
a. decent housing;
b. health and social security;
c. population administration registration;
d. opportunities to obtain skills to improve economic status; and
e. access to information and legal consultation services.

